          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is defined in terms of certain classes of 2d
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            %em types
            of string theory.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                %em type II string theory
                the CFTs in question are
                = succeed "-supersymmetric" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %li
              %p
                In
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
                the CFTs in question are
                = succeed "-supersymmetric" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %li
              %p In …
          %p
            The Type II string theory referred to here is defined on spaces in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+geometry"} Lorentzian geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II%2A+string+theory"} type II* string theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-Lorentzian+type+II+string+theory"} non-Lorentzian type II string theory
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#effective_qft Effective QFT
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            of type II string theory containts –besides
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            and
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
          %h3#anomaly_cancellation Anomaly cancellation
          %p
            Apart from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+anomaly"} Weyl anomaly
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            also in general has an
            %em
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly#AnomalousActionFunctional"} anomalous action functional
            , for two reasons:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} higher holonomy
                of the higher
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                of a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            %li
              %p
                The fermionic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
                over the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
                produces as section of a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfaffian+line+bundle"} Pfaffian line bundle
          %p
            In order for the action functional to be well-defined, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of these different anomaly
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            must have trivial class (as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} bundles with connection
          %p
            For the open
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string"} type II string
            the condition is known as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freed-Witten+anomaly+cancellation"} Freed-Witten anomaly cancellation
            condition: it says that the restriction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            must consistute the twist of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+spin%5Ec+structure"} twisted spin^c structure
            on the brane.
          %p
            A more detailed analysis of these type II anomalies is in (
            %a{:href => "#DFMI"}> DFMI
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#DFMII"}> DFMII
            ).
